er  it's just ripley in that single  xenomorph in the original film or the  most recent cast of colonial characters  introduced in 2017 s alien covenant it's  usually a certainty that there will be  plenty of jump-scares grizzly chess  bursters galore and lots and lots of  Prometheus fails on a number of levels  but it's that lack of any real dread  that really makes it the most skippable  of alien movies modern franchises may  love to fill their universes with  obscure and often unnecessary origin  stories and that may work for running up  box office numbers but it doesn't make  the movies themselves any more  interesting filled with backstory yet  unable to strike that sense of fear  which makes alien movies so great  Prometheus just ends up feeling bloated  if you're a star Wars fan you're likely  familiar with the machete order a  viewing plan devised by rod Hilton in  which the prequels slot into the  original trilogy right after the Empire  Strikes Back  instead of attacked on afterthought the  prequels suddenly become a long  flashback sequence with far greater  emotional resonance than they originally  had notably the machete order amidst the  Phantom Menace the movie doesn't really  contain any critical story points  relevant to the rise and fall of Anakin  Skywalker or his eventual Redemption so  out it goes whatever order you do watch  these films in it's very easy to omit  the first Star Wars prequel without  missing any important story at all of  course it doesn't help that the Phantom  Menace is also a completely horrible  movie chances are it's not only as bad  as you remember it it's actually worse  just skip it Terminator Genisys was  envisioned as a fresh start for the  Terminator franchise arriving six years  after 2009's Terminator Salvation which  is today probably best remembered morph  of Christian Bale's on set rants than  for the movie itself Thor the dark world  Alan Taylor stepped up to direct joined  by Game of Thrones star Emilia Clarke 